[THOUGHT] A normal CA-125 level significantly challenges the diagnosis of epithelial ovarian cancer — which typically presents with markedly elevated levels (>200 U/mL, often >500 U/mL in advanced disease). While CA-125 can be normal in early-stage or non-serous subtypes (e.g., mucinous, low-grade), it is uncommon with *large ovarian mass + significant ascites + pleural effusion*. This strongly raises suspicion for **Meigs’ syndrome**, a benign condition classically defined by:  
- Triad of ovarian fibroma (or other benign tumor), ascites, and pleural effusion;  
- Resolution of fluid collections after surgical removal of the tumor;  
- Normal or only mildly elevated CA-125.  
The physical and imaging findings align perfectly: large right ovarian mass, ascites, right pleural effusion, no other systemic signs. Meigs’ syndrome is rare but remains the best fit given the normal CA-125 — and it explains all findings without invoking malignancy. Other mimics (e.g., gastrointestinal stromal tumor, metastatic disease) would not typically spare CA-125. Thus, the single most likely diagnosis is now Meigs’ syndrome.

[DIAGNOSIS] Meigs’ syndrome

